      <p class="layer-description">Before AI systems can say anything about your business, they need to be able to reach your website. This dimension checks whether your technical infrastructure welcomes or blocks AI crawlers, and whether you've set up the specific files and configurations that AI models look for. For Marq, this also covers whether the <strong>capability surface</strong> — the MCP server and public API at developers.marq.com — is discoverable from the brand home at all.</p>
    </div>

    <!-- AI Crawler Access Topic Group -->
    <div class="findings-section">
      <h3>AI Crawler Access</h3>
      <p class="topic-intro">AI crawlers are the automated systems that models send to read your website. If crawlers can't get in, AI can't learn about you. These checks verify that your technical infrastructure welcomes AI traffic.</p>

      <!-- PASS finding: robots.txt -->
      <details>
        <summary><span class="badge pass">PASS</span> robots.txt Allows AI Crawlers</summary>
        <div class="finding-content">
          <p style="margin-bottom: 12px;">A robots.txt file sits at the root of your website and tells web crawlers (including AI systems) which pages they can and cannot access. It is the first thing any AI model checks before reading your site.</p>

          <details style="margin-top: 12px;">
            <summary style="font-weight: 500; font-size: 14px;">What we found</summary>
            <div class="finding-content">
              <p style="margin-bottom: 12px;">Marq's robots.txt is permissive. The default rule (User-Agent: *) allows all crawlers full access. No AI crawlers are blocked. This is good. Your front door is open.</p>

              <details style="margin-top: 12px;">
                <summary style="font-weight: 500; font-size: 13px;">Technical detail</summary>
                <div class="finding-content">
                  <p>Raw robots.txt at www.marq.com/robots.txt contains only a sitemap reference and a permissive wildcard rule. No Disallow directives target named AI user-agents. The developers.marq.com subdomain serves its own robots.txt, also permissive.</p>
                </div>
              </details>
            </div>
          </details>
        </div>
      </details>

      <!-- MEDIUM finding: Selective crawler management -->
      <details>
        <summary><span class="badge medium">MEDIUM</span> No Explicit AI Crawler Directives <span style="font-size: 12px; color: var(--text-secondary); font-weight: 400;">(BV-011)</span></summary>
        <div class="finding-content">
          <p style="margin-bottom: 12px;">Modern AI platforms each send their own named crawler to read your website. Explicitly allowing these crawlers by name (GPTBot for OpenAI, PerplexityBot, Google-Extended, ClaudeBot) signals that your site welcomes AI indexing and can improve crawl priority.</p>

          <details style="margin-top: 12px;">
            <summary style="font-weight: 500; font-size: 14px;">What we found</summary>
            <div class="finding-content">
              <p style="margin-bottom: 12px;">Marq's robots.txt has no explicit rules for any AI crawler. GPTBot, ClaudeBot, PerplexityBot, Google-Extended, Bytespider, and CCBot all fall under the permissive wildcard. They will crawl the site, but there is no explicit signal that Marq intends to be AI-readable — and no way to differentiate treatment between a design-template SaaS brand and a generic marketing site.</p>

              <details style="margin-top: 12px;">
                <summary style="font-weight: 500; font-size: 13px;">Technical detail</summary>
                <div class="finding-content">
                  <p>AI crawlers not specifically addressed on www.marq.com: GPTBot, Google-Extended, Bytespider, CCBot, Anthropic-AI, ClaudeBot, PerplexityBot, Amazonbot. All fall under User-Agent: * (permissive). Same pattern on developers.marq.com — no explicit AI-crawler Allow rules to flag the capability surface as especially machine-relevant.</p>
                </div>
              </details>

              <p style="margin-bottom: 12px; padding: 12px; background-color: var(--cream); border-left: 3px solid var(--accent-copper); border-radius: 4px; font-style: italic; color: var(--text-secondary);"><strong>Recommendation:</strong> Redacted for this sanitized sample.</p>
            </div>
          </details>
        </div>
      </details>
    </div>

    <!-- Discovery & Capability Surface Topic Group -->
    <div class="findings-section">
      <h3>Discovery &amp; Capability Surface</h3>
      <p class="topic-intro">Reaching your home page is only step one. For a SaaS company with a developer-addressable surface (MCP, API, webhooks), AI agents also need a path from the brand home to the capability surface. Without that path, the capability is invisible even when it exists.</p>

      <!-- HIGH finding: Headless capability surface -->
      <details>
        <summary><span class="badge high">HIGH</span> Headless Capability Surface: No Discovery Path to developers.marq.com <span style="font-size: 12px; color: var(--text-secondary); font-weight: 400;">(BV-001)</span></summary>
        <div class="finding-content">
          <p style="margin-bottom: 12px;">Marq has two things most of its competitors do not: a published MCP server and a public API at <strong>developers.marq.com</strong>. This is a structural advantage for the agentic era — an AI agent that knows about Marq's MCP can call Marq directly to generate, brand-check, or distribute content on behalf of a user. But an agent can only use a capability it can discover, and there is no discovery path from www.marq.com to developers.marq.com today.</p>

          <details style="margin-top: 12px;">
            <summary style="font-weight: 500; font-size: 14px;">What we found</summary>
            <div class="finding-content">
              <p style="margin-bottom: 12px;">Three independent gaps all reinforce the same problem:</p>
              <ul style="margin: 0 0 12px 24px; line-height: 1.8;">
                <li>The Marq homepage global navigation has no link labeled Developers, API, Integrations, or Build. The capability surface is not in the IA.</li>
                <li>The Organization schema block on www.marq.com lists six sameAs links (LinkedIn, X, Facebook, YouTube, Instagram, Crunchbase) but does not include developers.marq.com. A machine reading the schema will not learn the two domains belong to the same entity.</li>
                <li>There is no /developers or /api path on www.marq.com that 301-redirects or soft-links to the subdomain. A crawler that starts at the root sees no signal the subdomain exists.</li>
              </ul>
              <p style="margin-bottom: 12px;">The net effect: an AI agent asked "which design-template platforms expose an MCP or API I can call?" cannot connect Marq to its own answer. The capability is live but unclaimed.</p>

              <details style="margin-top: 12px;">
                <summary style="font-weight: 500; font-size: 13px;">Technical detail</summary>
                <div class="finding-content">
                  <p style="margin-bottom: 8px;">www.marq.com homepage Organization JSON-LD sameAs array: [linkedin.com/company/marq, x.com/marq, facebook.com/marq, youtube.com/marq, instagram.com/marq, crunchbase.com/organization/marq]. Zero entries for developers.marq.com.</p>
                  <p style="margin-bottom: 8px;">www.marq.com global nav (as rendered): Product, Solutions, Templates, Pricing, Customers, Resources, Log in, Book demo. No Developers/API/Integrations entry.</p>
                  <p>developers.marq.com responds 200 OK and has its own schema and sitemap, confirming the capability surface is fully live. The problem is purely a discovery/linkage failure, not a technology failure.</p>
                </div>
              </details>

              <p style="margin-bottom: 12px; padding: 12px; background-color: rgba(91, 124, 94, 0.08); border-left: 3px solid var(--accent-teal); border-radius: 4px; font-size: 14px;"><strong>Behavioral corroboration:</strong> <em>Supporting AI-visibility benchmark data redacted for this sanitized sample.</em></p>

              <p style="margin-bottom: 12px; padding: 12px; background-color: var(--cream); border-left: 3px solid var(--accent-copper); border-radius: 4px; font-style: italic; color: var(--text-secondary);"><strong>Recommendation:</strong> Redacted for this sanitized sample.</p>
            </div>
          </details>
        </div>
      </details>

      <!-- HIGH finding: No agent discovery signals -->
      <details id="BV-016">
        <summary><span class="badge high">HIGH</span> Agent Discovery Signals Absent Despite MCP Server Existing <span style="font-size: 12px; color: var(--text-secondary); font-weight: 400;">(BV-016)</span></summary>
        <div class="finding-content">
          <p style="margin-bottom: 12px;">The agentic web has a small, specific set of files and link elements that tell AI agents "this site exposes callable capabilities, and here's where to find them." They are the digital equivalent of a business card for machines. Marq operates an MCP server at developers.marq.com — exactly the kind of capability these signals exist to advertise — but publishes none of them on www.marq.com.</p>

          <details style="margin-top: 12px;">
            <summary style="font-weight: 500; font-size: 14px;">What we found</summary>
            <div class="finding-content">
              <p style="margin-bottom: 12px;">Wordlift's agent-discovery scan returned "Not Found" for every standard discovery signal on www.marq.com:</p>
              <ul style="margin: 0 0 12px 24px; line-height: 1.8;">
                <li><code>&lt;link rel="mcp"&gt;</code> in the page head &mdash; not present</li>
                <li><code>/.well-known/mcp.json</code> &mdash; not present</li>
                <li><code>/.well-known/webmcp/tools.json</code> &mdash; not present</li>
                <li><code>/.well-known/mcp/server-card.json</code> &mdash; not present</li>
                <li><code>/.well-known/agent-skills/index.json</code> &mdash; not present</li>
              </ul>
              <p style="margin-bottom: 12px;">This is the machine-layer counterpart to finding BV-001. BV-001 covers the human/schema-layer invisibility (no "Developers" nav link, no sameAs entry, no /developers path). BV-016 covers the agent-layer invisibility (no manifest files any MCP-aware client would check). Together they mean an AI agent has <em>no path at all</em> from marq.com to the capability surface &mdash; neither by reading the page nor by checking the standard discovery endpoints.</p>

              <details style="margin-top: 12px;">
                <summary style="font-weight: 500; font-size: 13px;">Technical detail</summary>
                <div class="finding-content">
                  <p style="margin-bottom: 8px;">Standard discovery endpoints checked: <code>/.well-known/mcp.json</code>, <code>/.well-known/webmcp/tools.json</code>, <code>/.well-known/mcp/server-card.json</code>, <code>/.well-known/agent-skills/index.json</code>. All return 404.</p>
                  <p style="margin-bottom: 8px;">Page head inspection on homepage, Product, Platform, Pricing, and Marq AI: zero <code>&lt;link rel="mcp"&gt;</code> or <code>&lt;link rel="agent-skills"&gt;</code> elements.</p>
                  <p>developers.marq.com hosts the MCP implementation but does not publish a discovery manifest at its own <code>/.well-known/</code> path either &mdash; so even an agent that knows the subdomain exists has to hand-parse the docs rather than read a manifest.</p>
                </div>
              </details>

              <p style="margin-bottom: 12px; padding: 12px; background-color: var(--cream); border-left: 3px solid var(--accent-copper); border-radius: 4px; font-style: italic; color: var(--text-secondary);"><strong>Recommendation:</strong> Redacted for this sanitized sample.</p>
            </div>
          </details>
        </div>
      </details>

      <!-- HIGH finding: CAPTCHA blocks agents -->
      <details id="BV-017">
        <summary><span class="badge high">HIGH</span> CAPTCHA on Marketing Site Blocks the Same Agents Marq's MCP Invites <span style="font-size: 12px; color: var(--text-secondary); font-weight: 400;">(BV-017)</span></summary>
        <div class="finding-content">
          <p style="margin-bottom: 12px;">Wordlift's automation-readiness scan returned a single P1 (block-level) issue: a CAPTCHA is active on www.marq.com page bodies. CAPTCHAs are, by design, built to stop machines. Any AI agent attempting to complete a form, submit an inquiry, or follow an interactive flow on the marketing site will be blocked at the CAPTCHA step.</p>

          <details style="margin-top: 12px;">
            <summary style="font-weight: 500; font-size: 14px;">What we found</summary>
            <div class="finding-content">
              <p style="margin-bottom: 12px;">The structural irony matters more than the CAPTCHA itself. Marq operates an MCP server at developers.marq.com whose entire purpose is to let agents call Marq on behalf of a user &mdash; generate a doc, apply a brand kit, push to a channel. On the marketing site, the same agents are stopped at a CAPTCHA before they can even submit a book-a-demo form. The front door says "no bots." The side door says "call our API, bots welcome." An agent arriving cold cannot reconcile that.</p>

              <details style="margin-top: 12px;">
                <summary style="font-weight: 500; font-size: 13px;">Technical detail</summary>
                <div class="finding-content">
                  <p style="margin-bottom: 8px;">Wordlift flagged this as a P1 automation blocker: "A CAPTCHA was detected on the page. CAPTCHAs are designed to block bots and will prevent AI agents from interacting with the page, especially forms."</p>
                  <p>The CAPTCHA is likely bound to the book-demo and contact flows (anti-spam hygiene on lead capture). It was not designed with agent-initiated inquiry in mind &mdash; a reasonable 2023 assumption that has aged poorly.</p>
                </div>
              </details>

              <p style="margin-bottom: 12px; padding: 12px; background-color: var(--cream); border-left: 3px solid var(--accent-copper); border-radius: 4px; font-style: italic; color: var(--text-secondary);"><strong>Recommendation:</strong> Redacted for this sanitized sample.</p>
            </div>
          </details>
        </div>
      </details>

      <!-- INFO note: llms.txt status update -->
      <details>
        <summary><span class="badge low">INFO</span> llms.txt: Not a Required Standard <span style="font-size: 12px; color: var(--text-secondary); font-weight: 400;">(BV-012)</span></summary>
        <div class="finding-content">
          <p style="margin-bottom: 12px;"><strong>Update:</strong> llms.txt was proposed in 2024 as a plain-text file AI models could read for a structured site summary. Since then, the major AI crawlers — OpenAI/GPTBot, Anthropic/ClaudeBot, Google/Google-Extended, and Perplexity — have not adopted the standard. None of them fetch or use llms.txt today.</p>

          <p style="margin-bottom: 12px;">We flagged this file as "missing" in earlier versions of this audit. That guidance has been retired. You do not need to create an llms.txt file, and if one were added it would not change how AI platforms describe Marq.</p>

          <p style="margin-bottom: 12px; padding: 12px; background-color: var(--cream); border-left: 3px solid var(--accent-copper); border-radius: 4px; font-style: italic; color: var(--text-secondary);"><strong>Recommendation:</strong> Redacted for this sanitized sample.</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <!-- LOW finding: Cloudflare Markdown -->
      <details>
        <summary><span class="badge low">LOW</span> Cloudflare Markdown for Agents Not Configured <span style="font-size: 12px; color: var(--text-secondary); font-weight: 400;">(BV-015)</span></summary>
        <div class="finding-content">
          <p style="margin-bottom: 12px;">Cloudflare (Marq's CDN provider) offers a feature called "Markdown for Agents" that automatically reformats pages into clean, structured markdown when an AI crawler requests them. This makes content significantly easier for AI models to parse and understand — especially useful on image-heavy, JS-heavy pages like a template-preview site.</p>

          <details style="margin-top: 12px;">
            <summary style="font-weight: 500; font-size: 14px;">What we found</summary>
            <div class="finding-content">
              <p style="margin-bottom: 12px;">The feature is not currently enabled. www.marq.com serves raw HTML to AI crawlers, which requires them to strip out navigation, JS, styling, and the image-heavy template gallery to find the actual narrative content. Enabling Markdown for Agents is a low-effort, high-impact configuration change.</p>

              <details style="margin-top: 12px;">
                <summary style="font-weight: 500; font-size: 13px;">Technical detail</summary>
                <div class="finding-content">
                  <p>Response headers confirm Cloudflare with cache_status: DYNAMIC (not cached at edge). cf-ray header present; cf-markdown-for-agents not present. Markdown for Agents was introduced February 2026 and is a dashboard toggle, not a code change.</p>
                </div>
              </details>

              <p style="margin-bottom: 12px; padding: 12px; background-color: var(--cream); border-left: 3px solid var(--accent-copper); border-radius: 4px; font-style: italic; color: var(--text-secondary);"><strong>Recommendation:</strong> Redacted for this sanitized sample.</p>
            </div>
          </details>
        </div>
      </details>
    </div>

    <!-- Sitemap and Infrastructure Topic Group -->
    <div class="findings-section">
      <h3>Sitemap and Infrastructure</h3>
      <p class="topic-intro">Sitemaps, CDN configuration, and metadata freshness ensure AI crawlers can efficiently find and trust what they read. These are the technical foundations that support everything else.</p>

      <!-- PASS finding: Sitemap -->
      <details>
        <summary><span class="badge pass">PASS</span> Sitemap Exists and Is Current</summary>
        <div class="finding-content">
          <p style="margin-bottom: 12px;">An XML sitemap tells search engines and AI crawlers which pages on your site exist, when they were last updated, and how to find them. It is essential for comprehensive indexing.</p>

          <details style="margin-top: 12px;">
            <summary style="font-weight: 500; font-size: 14px;">What we found</summary>
            <div class="finding-content">
              <p style="margin-bottom: 12px;">Marq has a sitemap at www.marq.com/sitemap.xml that lists all primary marketing pages and links through to a case-studies sitemap and a template-gallery sitemap. developers.marq.com maintains its own separate sitemap for API reference and docs content. Both are well-formed and referenced in the corresponding robots.txt.</p>

              <details style="margin-top: 12px;">
                <summary style="font-weight: 500; font-size: 13px;">Technical detail</summary>
                <div class="finding-content">
                  <p>Primary sitemap at https://www.marq.com/sitemap.xml. Sub-sitemaps include the marketing page set, the case-studies content type, and the template library. One observation: the two sitemaps (www and developers) are not cross-referenced, which reinforces the capability-surface discoverability gap flagged in finding BV-001.</p>
                </div>
              </details>
            </div>
          </details>
        </div>
      </details>

      <!-- PASS finding: Cloudflare CDN -->
      <details>
        <summary><span class="badge pass">PASS</span> Cloudflare CDN Detected</summary>
        <div class="finding-content">
          <p style="margin-bottom: 12px;">A Content Delivery Network (CDN) serves your website from servers around the world, ensuring fast load times for both human visitors and AI crawlers. Fast, reliable access improves crawl success rates.</p>

          <details style="margin-top: 12px;">
            <summary style="font-weight: 500; font-size: 14px;">What we found</summary>
            <div class="finding-content">
              <p>Marq runs on Cloudflare, one of the most AI-friendly CDN providers. Cloudflare supports AI-specific features including bot management, Markdown for Agents, and crawler analytics — all of which are available to enable on the existing plan.</p>
            </div>
          </details>
        </div>
      </details>

      <!-- MEDIUM finding: dateModified -->
      <details>
        <summary><span class="badge medium">MEDIUM</span> dateModified Freshness Signals Inconsistent Across Templates <span style="font-size: 12px; color: var(--text-secondary); font-weight: 400;">(SCH-007)</span></summary>
        <div class="finding-content">
          <p style="margin-bottom: 12px;">The dateModified field in structured data tells AI models when content was last updated. AI systems use this as a freshness signal — more recently updated content tends to get higher confidence in recommendations, and a missing or stale dateModified reads as "unmaintained."</p>

          <details style="margin-top: 12px;">
            <summary style="font-weight: 500; font-size: 14px;">What we found</summary>
            <div class="finding-content">
              <p style="margin-bottom: 12px;">Schema-level dateModified handling on marq.com is inconsistent across templates. The Pricing page, Platform page, and Marq AI page emit no dateModified at all (a by-product of emitting no Product, SoftwareApplication, or Offer schema — see UNDERSTAND findings). The case study template does emit a dateModified but reflects the CMS publish date rather than the most recent edit. Only the blog template (WordPress-native) handles dateModified correctly.</p>

              <details style="margin-top: 12px;">
                <summary style="font-weight: 500; font-size: 13px;">Technical detail</summary>
                <div class="finding-content">
                  <p>On the 6 sampled templates: homepage emits a dateModified on the Organization node that has not changed across multiple audit passes; Pricing/Platform/Marq AI emit no dateModified because no time-bound schema type is present; Case study emits dateModified from CMS publish field, not last-edit; About and blog handle it correctly. Fix lives downstream of the schema-coverage fixes in UNDERSTAND — when Product/Offer/SoftwareApplication schema is added, ensure the rendering template binds dateModified to the actual last-edit timestamp.</p>
                </div>
              </details>

              <p style="margin-bottom: 12px; padding: 12px; background-color: var(--cream); border-left: 3px solid var(--accent-copper); border-radius: 4px; font-style: italic; color: var(--text-secondary);"><strong>Recommendation:</strong> Redacted for this sanitized sample.</p>
            </div>
          </details>
        </div>
      </details>
