  <!-- UNDERSTAND Dimension Section -->
  <div class="container">
    <div class="layer-header">
      <div class="layer-header-top">
        <h3>Understand: Does AI Get What You Do?</h3>
        <div class="badges">
          <span class="count-badge high">12</span>
          <span class="count-badge medium">9</span>
          <span class="count-badge low">3</span>
          <span class="count-badge pass">2</span>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="layer-description">Once AI systems can access your site, the question becomes: do they understand what they are reading? This dimension checks whether your website communicates clearly in the language machines rely on &mdash; structured data, semantic markup, and content that answers the questions buyers ask. For Marq, the central pattern across this layer is the same one that appears in Find: the narrative prose is clear to humans, but the <strong>machine signals</strong> &mdash; Product schema on pricing, SoftwareApplication schema on the platform page, LLM-integration markers on Marq AI, CaseStudy schema on customer stories &mdash; are absent.</p>
    </div>

    <!-- Schema Implementation Topic Group -->
    <div class="findings-section">
      <h3>Schema Implementation</h3>
      <p class="topic-intro">Schema markup is the machine-readable vocabulary that tells AI exactly what each page represents. Pricing pages need Product / Offer schema. Product pages need SoftwareApplication. Case studies need Article or CaseStudy. Without these explicit labels, AI must guess what each page is about &mdash; and on a design-template SaaS site, the default guess is "generic marketing page."</p>

      <!-- HIGH: Pricing Zero Product/Offer Schema -->
      <details id="SCH-002">
        <summary><span class="badge high">HIGH</span> Pricing Page Emits Zero Product / Offer Schema <span style="font-size: 12px; color: var(--text-secondary); font-weight: 400;">(SCH-002)</span></summary>
        <div class="finding-content">
          <p style="margin-bottom: 12px;">The pricing page is the single highest-value page on a SaaS site for schema markup. Product schema identifies the offering, Offer schema carries price, currency, billing interval, and availability. When both are present, AI answer engines can confidently say "Marq's Pro tier is $20/user/month" with a citation. When both are absent, the same question produces either silence, hallucinated pricing, or a recommendation to "visit the pricing page."</p>

          <details style="margin-top: 12px;">
            <summary style="font-weight: 500; font-size: 14px;">What we found</summary>
            <div class="finding-content">
              <p style="margin-bottom: 12px;">The Marq pricing page displays four named tiers with visible prices and feature lists in the rendered DOM. The JSON-LD block on the same page contains Organization and WebPage. Zero Product nodes. Zero Offer nodes. Zero PriceSpecification. The page teaches a human what Marq costs; it teaches a machine nothing.</p>

              <details style="margin-top: 12px;">
                <summary style="font-weight: 500; font-size: 13px;">Technical detail</summary>
                <div class="finding-content">
                  <p>JSON-LD types present on www.marq.com/pricing: Organization, WebPage, BreadcrumbList. Missing: Product, Offer, AggregateOffer, PriceSpecification. Visible content lists four tiers with monthly prices, seat counts, and feature differentiators &mdash; all of which map cleanly to the Offer schema vocabulary.</p>
                </div>
              </details>

              <p style="margin-bottom: 12px; padding: 12px; background-color: var(--cream); border-left: 3px solid var(--accent-copper); border-radius: 4px; font-style: italic; color: var(--text-secondary);"><strong>Recommendation:</strong> Redacted for this sanitized sample.</p>
            </div>
          </details>
        </div>
      </details>

      <!-- HIGH: Platform Zero SoftwareApplication Schema -->
      <details id="SCH-005">
        <summary><span class="badge high">HIGH</span> Platform Page Emits Zero SoftwareApplication Schema <span style="font-size: 12px; color: var(--text-secondary); font-weight: 400;">(SCH-005)</span></summary>
        <div class="finding-content">
          <p style="margin-bottom: 12px;">SoftwareApplication is the schema type AI models look for when they need to classify a product as software. It carries applicationCategory, operatingSystem, featureList, and (critically for trust) aggregateRating. On a SaaS platform page, it is the core entity declaration &mdash; the machine equivalent of "this is the product."</p>

          <details style="margin-top: 12px;">
            <summary style="font-weight: 500; font-size: 14px;">What we found</summary>
            <div class="finding-content">
              <p style="margin-bottom: 12px;">The Platform page describes Marq as a brand-templating and design-distribution platform, with named capabilities (brand locks, bulk template editing, asset library, analytics). The JSON-LD block declares Organization and WebPage. There is no SoftwareApplication node, no applicationCategory, no featureList array, no aggregateRating. A machine crawling this page cannot confirm that "Marq" is a software product as opposed to a consulting service or a physical good.</p>

              <details style="margin-top: 12px;">
                <summary style="font-weight: 500; font-size: 13px;">Technical detail</summary>
                <div class="finding-content">
                  <p>JSON-LD on www.marq.com/platform: Organization, WebPage, BreadcrumbList. Missing: SoftwareApplication, applicationCategory (should be "DesignApplication" or "BusinessApplication"), operatingSystem ("Web"), featureList, aggregateRating. The rendered page would populate a SoftwareApplication schema straightforwardly &mdash; the feature list is already written in prose.</p>
                </div>
              </details>

              <p style="margin-bottom: 12px; padding: 12px; background-color: var(--cream); border-left: 3px solid var(--accent-copper); border-radius: 4px; font-style: italic; color: var(--text-secondary);"><strong>Recommendation:</strong> Redacted for this sanitized sample.</p>
            </div>
          </details>
        </div>
      </details>

      <!-- HIGH: 16 Case Studies Zero Schema -->
      <details id="SCH-004">
        <summary><span class="badge high">HIGH</span> 16 Case Studies Emit Zero Article / CaseStudy / Review Schema <span style="font-size: 12px; color: var(--text-secondary); font-weight: 400;">(SCH-004)</span></summary>
        <div class="finding-content">
          <p style="margin-bottom: 12px;">Case studies are the single strongest trust signal a SaaS site can offer. For AI to cite them, they need schema: Article or CaseStudy to classify the page, about.Organization to name the customer, result / mentions to surface outcome metrics, and optionally Review with reviewRating to carry the customer's voice. Without schema, a case study is prose &mdash; readable but not quotable in structured form.</p>

          <details style="margin-top: 12px;">
            <summary style="font-weight: 500; font-size: 14px;">What we found</summary>
            <div class="finding-content">
              <p style="margin-bottom: 12px;">Marq's customer-stories content library contains 16 case studies that appear in the sitemap and render correctly to humans. The sampled case study template emits Organization and WebPage JSON-LD. Zero Article, Zero CaseStudy, Zero Review, Zero about fields, zero mentions of the customer organization, zero numeric result fields. AI agents asked "which brand-marketing teams have reduced template production time with Marq?" cannot surface a cited customer &mdash; they can only hallucinate one or say they don't know.</p>

              <details style="margin-top: 12px;">
                <summary style="font-weight: 500; font-size: 13px;">Technical detail</summary>
                <div class="finding-content">
                  <p>Customer-stories sitemap lists 16 case study URLs. Sampled template: JSON-LD types = Organization, WebPage, BreadcrumbList. Missing: Article (or CaseStudy), headline, author (Person or Organization), about (the customer Organization), datePublished, publisher, result, and optionally Review + reviewRating if the quote-style testimonials qualify. Fix is template-level, not per-page: one template change updates all 16 case studies.</p>
                </div>
              </details>

              <p style="margin-bottom: 12px; padding: 12px; background-color: var(--cream); border-left: 3px solid var(--accent-copper); border-radius: 4px; font-style: italic; color: var(--text-secondary);"><strong>Recommendation:</strong> Redacted for this sanitized sample.</p>
            </div>
          </details>
        </div>
      </details>

      <!-- MEDIUM: About Page Missing Person Schema -->
      <details id="SCH-006">
        <summary><span class="badge medium" style="color: var(--slate);">MEDIUM</span> About Page Missing Person Schema for Leadership <span style="font-size: 12px; color: var(--text-secondary); font-weight: 400;">(SCH-006)</span></summary>
        <div class="finding-content">
          <p style="margin-bottom: 12px;">Person schema tells AI who leads your company, what their roles are, and where else they appear online. For a SaaS brand with a named founder and a small leadership team, Person schema on About is low-effort and high-value &mdash; it lets AI tie press coverage, conference talks, and LinkedIn profiles back to the company entity.</p>

          <details style="margin-top: 12px;">
            <summary style="font-weight: 500; font-size: 14px;">What we found</summary>
            <div class="finding-content">
              <p style="margin-bottom: 12px;">The About page names and pictures members of the Marq leadership team but emits zero Person nodes in JSON-LD. The team appears visually but is invisible to machine queries like "who is the CEO of Marq" or "who founded Marq." A complete fix adds one Person entity per named leader with jobTitle, worksFor (linked to the Marq Organization), and sameAs (LinkedIn, X, etc.).</p>

              <details style="margin-top: 12px;">
                <summary style="font-weight: 500; font-size: 13px;">Technical detail</summary>
                <div class="finding-content">
                  <p>JSON-LD on www.marq.com/about: Organization, WebPage, BreadcrumbList. Missing: Person nodes, worksFor linkage, sameAs arrays for individual leaders. Rendered DOM contains the names and titles &mdash; fix is template-level.</p>
                </div>
              </details>

              <p style="margin-bottom: 12px; padding: 12px; background-color: var(--cream); border-left: 3px solid var(--accent-copper); border-radius: 4px; font-style: italic; color: var(--text-secondary);"><strong>Recommendation:</strong> Redacted for this sanitized sample.</p>
            </div>
          </details>
        </div>
      </details>

      <!-- MEDIUM: About Missing BreadcrumbList -->
      <details id="SCH-008">
        <summary><span class="badge medium" style="color: var(--slate);">MEDIUM</span> About Page Missing BreadcrumbList Schema <span style="font-size: 12px; color: var(--text-secondary); font-weight: 400;">(SCH-008)</span></summary>
        <div class="finding-content">
          <p style="margin-bottom: 12px;">BreadcrumbList tells crawlers where a page sits in the site hierarchy. It is trivial to add and helps AI models assemble a mental map of the site &mdash; which is especially useful on a site where the About page is the hub for company-identity queries.</p>

          <details style="margin-top: 12px;">
            <summary style="font-weight: 500; font-size: 14px;">What we found</summary>
            <div class="finding-content">
              <p style="margin-bottom: 12px;">BreadcrumbList is present on most templates (Pricing, Platform, case studies) but missing from the About page specifically. Inconsistent application of a schema that the CMS clearly supports suggests a template-override somewhere in the About render path.</p>

              <p style="margin-bottom: 12px; padding: 12px; background-color: var(--cream); border-left: 3px solid var(--accent-copper); border-radius: 4px; font-style: italic; color: var(--text-secondary);"><strong>Recommendation:</strong> Redacted for this sanitized sample.</p>
            </div>
          </details>
        </div>
      </details>

      <!-- PASS: Organization Schema Present -->
      <details>
        <summary><span class="badge pass">PASS</span> Organization Schema Present on Homepage</summary>
        <div class="finding-content">
          <p style="margin-bottom: 12px;">The most fundamental schema type is Organization, which declares that the website represents a business entity. This is the starting point for all other structured data.</p>

          <details style="margin-top: 12px;">
            <summary style="font-weight: 500; font-size: 14px;">What we found</summary>
            <div class="finding-content">
              <p style="margin-bottom: 12px;">Marq correctly declares Organization schema on the homepage with name, url, logo, description, and a six-entry sameAs array. The foundation is solid. The gap &mdash; covered in the Find layer as BV-001 &mdash; is that developers.marq.com is missing from the sameAs list. Fix the BV-001 finding and this PASS becomes a complete foundation for the rest of the schema work.</p>
            </div>
          </details>
        </div>
      </details>
    </div>

    <!-- Semantic HTML & Heading Structure Topic Group -->
    <div class="findings-section">
      <h3>Semantic HTML &amp; Heading Structure</h3>
      <p class="topic-intro">Semantic HTML tags (main, article, section, h1-h6) give AI a structural map of your content. Heading hierarchy tells AI which ideas are primary and which are supporting. When headings flatten or semantic tags collapse to divs, the page still reads cleanly to humans but becomes opaque to machine parsers.</p>

      <!-- HIGH: Low Text-to-HTML Ratio -->
      <details>
        <summary><span class="badge high">HIGH</span> Text-to-HTML Ratio 1.1&ndash;2.1% Across Marketing Pages <span style="font-size: 12px; color: var(--text-secondary); font-weight: 400;">(SEM-001)</span></summary>
        <div class="finding-content">
          <p style="margin-bottom: 12px;">Text-to-HTML ratio measures how much of a page's delivered bytes are actual content versus layout, styling, and JavaScript. On a well-structured content page the ratio typically sits 15&ndash;25%. Below 5% signals that the page is delivering a heavy framework with a thin layer of content &mdash; which is the same experience a crawler has, just without the benefit of rendering the page visually.</p>

          <details style="margin-top: 12px;">
            <summary style="font-weight: 500; font-size: 14px;">What we found</summary>
            <div class="finding-content">
              <p style="margin-bottom: 12px;">Across the six sampled templates, text-to-HTML ratios ranged from 1.1% (homepage) to 2.1% (About). The Pricing, Platform, and Marq AI pages all came in under 1.5%. This reflects the React / Next.js build pattern &mdash; large JS bundles and extensive inline styling &mdash; combined with short body copy on each page. The practical effect: crawlers that do not execute JavaScript see very little content. Crawlers that do execute JavaScript still weight pages by substantive text density, and Marq's is low.</p>

              <p style="margin-bottom: 12px;"><strong>Corroborating signal:</strong> Wordlift's independent AI-readiness audit on www.marq.com returned a semantic-tag ratio of 1% &mdash; effectively the same figure arrived at from a different measurement path. Wordlift characterized the finding as "extremely low&hellip;relying heavily on generic <code>div</code> and <code>span</code> elements," which is the same structural pattern we observed in the template-level drill-down. Two independent scans, one shared conclusion.</p>

              <details style="margin-top: 12px;">
                <summary style="font-weight: 500; font-size: 13px;">Technical detail</summary>
                <div class="finding-content">
                  <p>Per-template text-to-HTML ratios: Homepage 1.1%, About 2.1%, Pricing 1.3%, Platform 1.4%, Marq AI 1.2%, Case Study 1.8%. Fixes fall into two buckets: (a) lengthen and deepen body copy on Pricing, Platform, and Marq AI; (b) reduce inline style weight by moving to external stylesheets where possible. The schema-coverage fixes in this layer also help indirectly by giving crawlers more structured content to index.</p>
                </div>
              </details>

              <p style="margin-bottom: 12px; padding: 12px; background-color: var(--cream); border-left: 3px solid var(--accent-copper); border-radius: 4px; font-style: italic; color: var(--text-secondary);"><strong>Recommendation:</strong> Redacted for this sanitized sample.</p>
            </div>
          </details>
        </div>
      </details>

      <!-- HIGH: Marq AI Hides Machine Signals -->
      <details id="SEM-002">
        <summary><span class="badge high">HIGH</span> Marq AI Product Page Hides Machine-Integration Signals <span style="font-size: 12px; color: var(--text-secondary); font-weight: 400;">(SEM-002)</span></summary>
        <div class="finding-content">
          <p style="margin-bottom: 12px;">The /marq-ai page is where a machine-aware prospect &mdash; a developer, an AI buyer, or an LLM crawling on behalf of a user &mdash; should be able to confirm that Marq plays well with AI systems. On a page literally titled "Marq AI," that confirmation should be unambiguous. Today it is absent.</p>

          <details style="margin-top: 12px;">
            <summary style="font-weight: 500; font-size: 14px;">What we found</summary>
            <div class="finding-content">
              <p style="margin-bottom: 12px;">The Marq AI page describes AI-assisted content generation for end users (write copy, generate images, auto-brand). It does not mention: LLM, API, MCP, Claude, GPT, Gemini, webhook, integration, developer, or "build with Marq." The page that should anchor Marq's agentic positioning positions Marq as an AI <em>consumer</em>, not an AI <em>surface</em>. A machine looking for "brand templating platforms I can call" finds nothing here to match against.</p>

              <details style="margin-top: 12px;">
                <summary style="font-weight: 500; font-size: 13px;">Technical detail</summary>
                <div class="finding-content">
                  <p>Word-frequency analysis of the Marq AI page body copy: zero occurrences of LLM, API, MCP, Claude, GPT, Gemini, webhook, SDK, or integration. Also zero outbound links to developers.marq.com &mdash; the exact page where those concepts live. This is the single most discoverable AI-named page on the site; it is the highest-leverage place to add a "Build with Marq" paragraph.</p>
                </div>
              </details>

              <p style="margin-bottom: 12px; padding: 12px; background-color: rgba(91, 124, 94, 0.08); border-left: 3px solid var(--accent-teal); border-radius: 4px; font-size: 14px;"><strong>Behavioral corroboration:</strong> <em>Supporting AI-visibility benchmark data redacted for this sanitized sample.</em></p>

              <p style="margin-bottom: 12px; padding: 12px; background-color: var(--cream); border-left: 3px solid var(--accent-copper); border-radius: 4px; font-style: italic; color: var(--text-secondary);"><strong>Recommendation:</strong> Redacted for this sanitized sample.</p>
            </div>
          </details>
        </div>
      </details>

      <!-- HIGH: Case Study Problem-Solution With No Structured Proof -->
      <details>
        <summary><span class="badge high">HIGH</span> Case Study Problem-Solution Narrative With No Structured Proof <span style="font-size: 12px; color: var(--text-secondary); font-weight: 400;">(SEM-006)</span></summary>
        <div class="finding-content">
          <p style="margin-bottom: 12px;">Case studies carry the problem-solution-result narrative well in prose, but AI models weight quantified outcomes heavily. A story that says "cut production time dramatically" reads as marketing copy; a story that says "cut production time from 6 weeks to 3 days, validated across 40 retail locations" reads as evidence. The difference is whether the page makes the numbers machine-extractable.</p>

          <details style="margin-top: 12px;">
            <summary style="font-weight: 500; font-size: 14px;">What we found</summary>
            <div class="finding-content">
              <p style="margin-bottom: 12px;">The sampled case study follows a clean problem-solution-result arc in prose. It includes quoted customer testimonials. It does not include: named quantified outcomes in machine-extractable form (time saved, cost reduced, revenue gained), numeric ranges, or schema bindings for any metric mentioned in the copy. An AI model weighting this against a competitor with numeric case studies will defer to the competitor.</p>

              <p style="margin-bottom: 12px; padding: 12px; background-color: var(--cream); border-left: 3px solid var(--accent-copper); border-radius: 4px; font-style: italic; color: var(--text-secondary);"><strong>Recommendation:</strong> Redacted for this sanitized sample.</p>
            </div>
          </details>
        </div>
      </details>

      <!-- HIGH: About We-Statements -->
      <details>
        <summary><span class="badge high">HIGH</span> About Page Leads With 24 "We" Statements Before Any Customer Outcome <span style="font-size: 12px; color: var(--text-secondary); font-weight: 400;">(SEM-005)</span></summary>
        <div class="finding-content">
          <p style="margin-bottom: 12px;">AI models building a company description lean heavily on the About page. Pages that lead with "we believe / we built / we are" for 200+ words before introducing a customer or an outcome are readable by humans but describe the company in terms that do not map to buyer queries. Buyers ask "what problem does this company solve," not "what does this company believe about itself."</p>

          <details style="margin-top: 12px;">
            <summary style="font-weight: 500; font-size: 14px;">What we found</summary>
            <div class="finding-content">
              <p style="margin-bottom: 12px;">The Marq About page contains 24 first-person-plural ("we") statements in the first half of the page before naming a specific customer segment, a specific problem solved, or a specific outcome delivered. The prose is well-written; the framing is inward. When an AI model is asked "what does Marq do for mid-market marketing teams," the About page offers company-philosophy language rather than audience-anchored answer content.</p>

              <p style="margin-bottom: 12px; padding: 12px; background-color: var(--cream); border-left: 3px solid var(--accent-copper); border-radius: 4px; font-style: italic; color: var(--text-secondary);"><strong>Recommendation:</strong> Redacted for this sanitized sample.</p>
            </div>
          </details>
        </div>
      </details>

      <!-- MEDIUM: Platform Flat Heading Hierarchy -->
      <details>
        <summary><span class="badge medium" style="color: var(--slate);">MEDIUM</span> Platform Page Uses Flat Heading Hierarchy <span style="font-size: 12px; color: var(--text-secondary); font-weight: 400;">(SEM-007)</span></summary>
        <div class="finding-content">
          <p style="margin-bottom: 12px;">Heading tags (h1&ndash;h6) build an outline the machine uses to understand page structure. A page with a single h1 and seven h2s in a row reads as a flat list of equal-weight ideas &mdash; which is rarely the true content structure.</p>

          <details style="margin-top: 12px;">
            <summary style="font-weight: 500; font-size: 14px;">What we found</summary>
            <div class="finding-content">
              <p style="margin-bottom: 12px;">The Platform page has one h1 ("Marq Platform") and nine h2s covering capabilities. No h3 sub-groupings. In prose the page does cluster capabilities into themes (content creation, brand control, distribution, analytics) but the heading structure does not reflect that grouping. The fix is small and template-level.</p>

              <p style="margin-bottom: 12px; padding: 12px; background-color: var(--cream); border-left: 3px solid var(--accent-copper); border-radius: 4px; font-style: italic; color: var(--text-secondary);"><strong>Recommendation:</strong> Redacted for this sanitized sample.</p>
            </div>
          </details>
        </div>
      </details>

      <!-- MEDIUM: Platform Lacks Audience-Specific Content -->
      <details>
        <summary><span class="badge medium" style="color: var(--slate);">MEDIUM</span> Platform Page Lacks Audience-Specific Sections <span style="font-size: 12px; color: var(--text-secondary); font-weight: 400;">(SEM-008)</span></summary>
        <div class="finding-content">
          <p style="margin-bottom: 12px;">A platform page that addresses "marketers" generically covers the floor but leaves upside. Marq's buyer map includes brand ops, field marketing, franchise marketing, and in-house creative teams &mdash; each with distinct use cases. Audience-specific content lets AI answer role-scoped queries ("best design tools for franchise marketing") with a Marq citation.</p>

          <details style="margin-top: 12px;">
            <summary style="font-weight: 500; font-size: 14px;">What we found</summary>
            <div class="finding-content">
              <p style="margin-bottom: 12px;">The Platform page addresses a single generalized "marketer" reader. It does not contain role-specific sections or named use cases by team type. The Solutions nav does carry some role pages, but they are not cross-linked from the Platform page, so a crawler entering at /platform sees one undifferentiated audience.</p>

              <p style="margin-bottom: 12px; padding: 12px; background-color: var(--cream); border-left: 3px solid var(--accent-copper); border-radius: 4px; font-style: italic; color: var(--text-secondary);"><strong>Recommendation:</strong> Redacted for this sanitized sample.</p>
            </div>
          </details>
        </div>
      </details>

      <!-- PASS: About Semantic Structure -->
      <details>
        <summary><span class="badge pass">PASS</span> About Page Uses Sound Semantic HTML Structure</summary>
        <div class="finding-content">
          <p style="margin-bottom: 12px;">Semantic HTML (main, article, section, header, footer) gives AI a clean structural map. When the semantic tags match the content's actual hierarchy, machines can identify primary content, skip navigation, and summarize accurately.</p>

          <details style="margin-top: 12px;">
            <summary style="font-weight: 500; font-size: 14px;">What we found</summary>
            <div class="finding-content">
              <p style="margin-bottom: 12px;">The About page uses a main tag wrapping primary content, article tags for major content units, and section tags for sub-groupings. Heading hierarchy is orderly (h1 &rarr; h2 &rarr; h3). This is the structural pattern the rest of the site should be audited against &mdash; the Platform, Pricing, and Marq AI pages show weaker semantic discipline.</p>
            </div>
          </details>
        </div>
      </details>

      <!-- MEDIUM: Internal linking collapse -->
      <details id="SEM-003">
        <summary><span class="badge medium" style="color: var(--slate);">MEDIUM</span> Internal Linking Collapse: 3 Internal Links of 211 Total <span style="font-size: 12px; color: var(--text-secondary); font-weight: 400;">(SEM-003)</span></summary>
        <div class="finding-content">
          <p style="margin-bottom: 12px;">Internal links do two things AI systems rely on: they define topical relationships between pages (this article is related to that product), and they distribute authority so the pages an organization considers important get weighted accordingly. When a crawler lands on a page and finds almost no internal links, it has no cue which other pages on the site continue the topic &mdash; so it treats each page as a topical island.</p>

          <details style="margin-top: 12px;">
            <summary style="font-weight: 500; font-size: 14px;">What we found</summary>
            <div class="finding-content">
              <p style="margin-bottom: 12px;">Wordlift's link audit on www.marq.com returned <strong>3 internal links out of 211 total links</strong>. The other 208 are external (social icons, customer logos, partner badges, a handful of review-site links). In practical terms, a crawler or AI agent landing on the homepage is routed outward more than 60 times before encountering a link that keeps it on marq.com. That's the inverse of what a healthy content site looks like.</p>

              <details style="margin-top: 12px;">
                <summary style="font-weight: 500; font-size: 13px;">Technical detail</summary>
                <div class="finding-content">
                  <p style="margin-bottom: 8px;">Wordlift classifies this as ❌ Poor: "With only 3 internal links out of 211 total links, the content fails to effectively connect related pages within the site. This significantly hinders user navigation, reduces page authority flow, and prevents search engines from fully understanding the site's structure and topic depth."</p>
                  <p>The likely cause is a React / Next.js build where most inter-page navigation is handled by client-side routing components that do not emit crawlable <code>&lt;a href&gt;</code> elements on the server-rendered page. The fix is a rendering-layer issue, not a content issue &mdash; the site has plenty of internally-relevant content to link <em>to</em>.</p>
                </div>
              </details>

              <p style="margin-bottom: 12px; padding: 12px; background-color: var(--cream); border-left: 3px solid var(--accent-copper); border-radius: 4px; font-style: italic; color: var(--text-secondary);"><strong>Recommendation:</strong> Redacted for this sanitized sample.</p>
            </div>
          </details>
        </div>
      </details>

      <!-- LOW: No Q&amp;A structure -->
      <details id="SEM-004">
        <summary><span class="badge low">LOW</span> Content Has No Q&amp;A-Style Headings for Direct-Answer Citation <span style="font-size: 12px; color: var(--text-secondary); font-weight: 400;">(SEM-004)</span></summary>
        <div class="finding-content">
          <p style="margin-bottom: 12px;">AI systems surface content in two modes: full-article summarization and direct-answer extraction. Direct-answer extraction &mdash; the mode behind featured snippets, voice-assistant responses, and conversational AI citations &mdash; relies heavily on Q&amp;A-style headings: a question phrased as a heading followed by a compact answer in the first sentence below. Content that uses only descriptive headings ("Templates for Every Team") gets read in full-article mode; content that uses question headings ("What is Marq?", "How much does Marq cost?") gets quoted verbatim.</p>

          <details style="margin-top: 12px;">
            <summary style="font-weight: 500; font-size: 14px;">What we found</summary>
            <div class="finding-content">
              <p style="margin-bottom: 12px;">Wordlift's content-structure analysis found 54 sections and 30 headings across www.marq.com with <em>zero</em> Q&amp;A-style headings. This means Marq's pages are readable in article-summary mode but have no anchors for featured-snippet or direct-answer citation. Competitors that publish FAQ-style content regularly capture these citations by default.</p>

              <p style="margin-bottom: 12px; padding: 12px; background-color: var(--cream); border-left: 3px solid var(--accent-copper); border-radius: 4px; font-style: italic; color: var(--text-secondary);"><strong>Recommendation:</strong> Redacted for this sanitized sample.</p>
            </div>
          </details>
        </div>
      </details>
    </div>

    <!-- Entity Clarity Topic Group -->
    <div class="findings-section">
      <h3>Entity Clarity</h3>
      <p class="topic-intro">Schema markup tells AI what each page <em>is</em>. Semantic HTML gives AI a structural map of each page. <strong>Entity Clarity</strong> is the third leg of UNDERSTAND: does the site's <em>page inventory</em> contain the pages a well-defined entity is expected to have? The framework underneath this section is the Lily Ray + Notebook Agency EEAT Page Framework for SaaS (42 expected pages across 11 categories). A site can have clean schema on every page it ships and still fail to establish the entity because the pages a machine needs &mdash; a Brand Facts file, an AI Policy, a System Status page, a developer surface discoverable from the brand home &mdash; simply do not exist. The findings below diagnose that inventory gap category by category; the roadmap sequences the work.</p>

