      <!-- EC-001: Entity definition & identity -->
      <details id="EC-001">
        <summary><span class="badge high">HIGH</span> Entity Definition &amp; Identity &mdash; Leadership Page Lacks Person Schema, No Company History, Ownership Unstated <span style="font-size: 12px; color: var(--text-secondary); font-weight: 400;">(EC-001)</span></summary>
        <div class="finding-content">
          <p style="margin-bottom: 12px;">A machine walking the entity-identification category on marq.com finds an About page, names five leaders, and stops. It cannot confirm when the company was founded from a canonical timeline, who owns the company, or how the leadership team's credentials tie back to the entity. The identity surface presents a company; it does not substantiate one.</p>

          <details style="margin-top: 12px;">
            <summary style="font-weight: 500; font-size: 14px;">What we found</summary>
            <div class="finding-content">
              <p style="margin-bottom: 12px;">Coverage 75% (3 of 4 Present or Partial), mean content quality 2.67, schema completeness 0%. The About page functions as both the company page and the leadership page &mdash; fine as a content decision, a problem as a machine-readability decision because the page emits WebPage schema and nothing else. No <code>Person</code> nodes for the executives whose names appear in the prose. No <code>foundingDate</code> on the Organization. No Corporate Structure / Ownership page disclosing the Charles Thayne Capital acquisition publicly on site (press coverage is the only record).</p>

              <div style="margin-top: 12px; font-size: 13px;">
                <strong>Per-page scoring</strong>
                <ul style="margin-top: 6px; line-height: 1.7;">
                  <li>About Us &middot; Present &middot; <a href="https://www.marq.com/about/">/about/</a> &middot; L2=3 &middot; no schema</li>
                  <li>Leadership / Team &middot; Present (as a section of About) &middot; L2=3 &middot; no Person schema</li>
                  <li>Company History / Timeline &middot; Partial (one paragraph on About) &middot; L2=2 &middot; no schema</li>
                  <li>Corporate Structure / Ownership &middot; Absent &middot; &mdash;</li>
                </ul>
              </div>

              <p style="margin-bottom: 12px;">When an AI model is asked "who runs Marq" or "when was Marq founded," the answer lives in prose on one page with no structured binding. Competitors with individual leadership pages and <code>Person</code> schema get cited first because the model has something to extract; Marq requires the model to read and summarize.</p>

              <p style="margin-bottom: 12px; padding: 12px; background-color: rgba(91, 124, 94, 0.08); border-left: 3px solid var(--accent-teal); border-radius: 4px; font-size: 14px;"><strong>Cross-references:</strong> <a href="#SEM-005">SEM-005</a> (About page leads with 24 "we" statements) reads the same About page through the semantic lens. <a href="#SCH-005">SCH-005</a> (no SoftwareApplication schema) reflects the same "WebPage-only schema everywhere" pattern.</p>

              <p style="margin-bottom: 12px; font-size: 13px; color: var(--text-secondary);"><strong>Severity reasoning:</strong> HIGH because entity definition is load-bearing for any SaaS audit &mdash; a machine that cannot establish who the company is cannot cite the company. <strong>Addressed in roadmap R-xxx.</strong></p>
            </div>
          </details>
        </div>
      </details>

      <!-- EC-002: Product & service understanding -->
      <details id="EC-002">
        <summary><span class="badge high">HIGH</span> Product &amp; Service Understanding &mdash; Integrations and Glossary Buried in Help Center, Comparison Content Lives in Blog <span style="font-size: 12px; color: var(--text-secondary); font-weight: 400;">(EC-002)</span></summary>
        <div class="finding-content">
          <p style="margin-bottom: 12px;">This category is 100% covered on paper: a page exists for each of the four expected roles. The issue is where those pages live. The Platform page is the only canonical, brand-domain, entity-anchored answer to "what does Marq sell." Integrations, product taxonomy / glossary, and competitive comparisons all live off the brand domain &mdash; in the help center, on the developer subdomain, or inside the blog. A machine looking to understand the product from canonical pages finds one page; everything else it has to stitch.</p>

          <details style="margin-top: 12px;">
            <summary style="font-weight: 500; font-size: 14px;">What we found</summary>
            <div class="finding-content">
              <p style="margin-bottom: 12px;">Coverage 100%, mean content quality 2.75, schema completeness 0%. Two of the four pages are Partial because the content exists but not at a canonical brand-home location.</p>

              <div style="margin-top: 12px; font-size: 13px;">
                <strong>Per-page scoring</strong>
                <ul style="margin-top: 6px; line-height: 1.7;">
                  <li>Platform / Product Overview &middot; Present &middot; <a href="https://www.marq.com/pages/platform">/pages/platform</a> &middot; L2=3 &middot; no SoftwareApplication schema</li>
                  <li>Integrations / Ecosystem &middot; Partial (on help.marq.com, not www) &middot; L2=3 &middot; no schema</li>
                  <li>Comparison / Alternatives &middot; Present (in /blog) &middot; L2=3 &middot; no FAQPage or Product schema</li>
                  <li>Product Taxonomy / Glossary &middot; Partial (help center, buried) &middot; L2=2 &middot; no DefinedTerm schema</li>
                </ul>
              </div>

              <p style="margin-bottom: 12px;">On the surface this looks like a content problem. Structurally it is an inventory problem: the pages exist, but their location pattern (help.marq.com, blog, no glossary surface) means a machine building a product understanding for Marq has to merge at least three subdomains into one entity picture. The schema gap makes that stitching worse &mdash; no page signals its role to the machine explicitly.</p>

              <p style="margin-bottom: 12px; padding: 12px; background-color: rgba(91, 124, 94, 0.08); border-left: 3px solid var(--accent-teal); border-radius: 4px; font-size: 14px;"><strong>Cross-references:</strong> <a href="#SCH-005">SCH-005</a> (no SoftwareApplication schema on Platform) addresses the same gap on one page through the structured-data lens. <a href="#SEM-002">SEM-002</a> (Marq AI page hides integration signals) is the Marq AI expression of the integrations-visibility gap.</p>

              <p style="margin-bottom: 12px; font-size: 13px; color: var(--text-secondary);"><strong>Severity reasoning:</strong> HIGH because product understanding is load-bearing for any SaaS entity and directly drives the "what is this" answer machines give. <strong>Addressed in roadmap R-xxx.</strong></p>
