      <summary><span class="badge high">HIGH</span> Case Study Titles Use Generic Marq-Centric H1s <span style="font-size: 12px; color: var(--text-secondary); font-weight: 400;">(SEM-004)</span></summary>
      <div class="finding-content">
        <p style="margin-bottom: 12px;">A case study H1 is the single text string an AI is most likely to surface verbatim when citing that study. "Customer Story: [Customer] ships X% faster with Marq" cites cleanly. "Marq Helps Brand Teams Scale" does not &mdash; there's no customer named, no outcome quantified, nothing for an answer engine to hang a recommendation on.</p>

        <details style="margin-top: 12px;">
          <summary style="font-weight: 500; font-size: 14px;">What we found</summary>
          <div class="finding-content">
            <p style="margin-bottom: 12px;">Sampled case study H1s follow a Marq-centric pattern: "Marq [verb]s [capability]" rather than "[Customer] achieved [outcome] with Marq." Combined with the missing schema, this means the two most-cited fields on a case study (the H1 string and the CaseStudy.headline schema property) both fail to surface customer identity or outcome. Twin fix: rewrite titles customer-first, then bind the customer-first title to schema headline.</p>

            <p style="margin-bottom: 12px; padding: 12px; background-color: var(--cream); border-left: 3px solid var(--accent-copper); border-radius: 4px; font-style: italic; color: var(--text-secondary);"><strong>Recommendation:</strong> Redacted for this sanitized sample.</p>
          </div>
        </details>
      </div>
    </details>

    <!-- HIGH: No Person-to-Content Authority Chain -->
    <details>
      <summary><span class="badge high">HIGH</span> No Person-to-Content Authority Chain <span style="font-size: 12px; color: var(--text-secondary); font-weight: 400;">(SCH-006 &mdash; Trust angle)</span></summary>
      <div class="finding-content">
        <p style="margin-bottom: 12px;">AI builds authority profiles by linking a Person (with credentials) to their content (blog posts, talks, case studies) to the Organization they represent. Marq has all three pieces &mdash; named leadership on /about, published blog posts, customer stories &mdash; but the chain is not wired. Each piece exists in isolation, so AI sees content without authors, leaders without published work, and an organization without quotable experts.</p>

        <details style="margin-top: 12px;">
          <summary style="font-weight: 500; font-size: 14px;">What we found</summary>
          <div class="finding-content">
            <p style="margin-bottom: 12px;">No Person schema on /about (see <a href="#SCH-006">SCH-006</a>). No author field on blog posts (no Article schema at all). No contributor links from case studies to named Marq employees. Trust consequence: when an AI model is asked "who at Marq talks about agentic content workflows," the model has no machine-readable answer even if the answer exists in prose somewhere on the site.</p>

            <p style="margin-bottom: 12px; padding: 12px; background-color: var(--cream); border-left: 3px solid var(--accent-copper); border-radius: 4px; font-style: italic; color: var(--text-secondary);"><strong>Recommendation:</strong> Redacted for this sanitized sample.</p>
          </div>
        </details>
      </div>
    </details>

    <!-- MEDIUM: G2 Rating Not In Schema -->
    <details>
      <summary><span class="badge medium" style="color: var(--slate);">MEDIUM</span> G2 Rating Exists Off-Site But Is Not Bound Into Product Schema <span style="font-size: 12px; color: var(--text-secondary); font-weight: 400;">(SCH-009)</span></summary>
      <div class="finding-content">
        <p style="margin-bottom: 12px;">Third-party review aggregators carry weight with AI systems because they are verifiable outside the company's own site. Marq has a strong G2 presence with hundreds of reviews and a public rating, but that rating lives only on G2 &mdash; it is not surfaced as an aggregateRating in Marq's own structured data. When an AI model builds a recommendation, it will find the G2 rating only if it happens to crawl G2 separately; it will not find it via Marq's own schema.</p>

        <details style="margin-top: 12px;">
          <summary style="font-weight: 500; font-size: 14px;">What we found</summary>
          <div class="finding-content">
            <p style="margin-bottom: 12px;">No aggregateRating property anywhere in Marq's schema graph. No Review schema. The G2 rating exists on g2.com/products/marq; it does not exist in machine-readable form anywhere on marq.com.</p>

            <p style="margin-bottom: 12px; padding: 12px; background-color: var(--cream); border-left: 3px solid var(--accent-copper); border-radius: 4px; font-style: italic; color: var(--text-secondary);"><strong>Recommendation:</strong> Redacted for this sanitized sample.</p>
          </div>
        </details>
      </div>
    </details>

    <!-- MEDIUM: Blog Lacks Authored Content Schema -->
    <details>
      <summary><span class="badge medium" style="color: var(--slate);">MEDIUM</span> Blog Lacks Article Schema, Breaking Thought-Leadership Trust <span style="font-size: 12px; color: var(--text-secondary); font-weight: 400;">(SCH-010)</span></summary>
      <div class="finding-content">
        <p style="margin-bottom: 12px;">Blog content signals thought leadership to AI systems &mdash; but only when the content is classified as authored, dated, and attributed. Plain WebPage schema on a blog post tells AI "this is a web page." Article schema tells AI "this is a published piece with an author, a publication date, and a topic." The difference matters for anything where AI has to decide whether to cite the source.</p>

        <details style="margin-top: 12px;">
          <summary style="font-weight: 500; font-size: 14px;">What we found</summary>
          <div class="finding-content">
            <p style="margin-bottom: 12px;">Blog posts on www.marq.com emit Organization + WebPage JSON-LD. No Article, no author, no datePublished, no headline. Given that Marq publishes regular product and industry content, this is leaving authority on the floor &mdash; AI has no reason to prefer a Marq post over a generic marketing source when both appear in search.</p>

            <p style="margin-bottom: 12px; padding: 12px; background-color: var(--cream); border-left: 3px solid var(--accent-copper); border-radius: 4px; font-style: italic; color: var(--text-secondary);"><strong>Recommendation:</strong> Redacted for this sanitized sample.</p>
          </div>
        </details>
      </div>
    </details>

    <!-- PASS: Organization Schema Foundation Present -->
    <details>
      <summary><span class="badge pass">PASS</span> Organization Schema Foundation Is Present</summary>
      <div class="finding-content">
        <p style="margin-bottom: 12px;">A trust layer built on nothing collapses. Trust built on a correct foundation extends easily. Marq's foundation is present.</p>

        <details style="margin-top: 12px;">
          <summary style="font-weight: 500; font-size: 14px;">What we found</summary>
          <div class="finding-content">
            <p style="margin-bottom: 12px;">Organization schema is correctly declared on the homepage and reused on child pages via BreadcrumbList references. The entity has a name, a URL, a logo, a description, and a populated (if incomplete) sameAs array. The fixes in this layer are additions to a valid foundation, not rebuilds. A machine already knows Marq is an Organization; the TRUST work is teaching it which Organization, with what proof, and with what reach.</p>
          </div>
        </details>
      </div>
    </details>
